    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Read `nums[2]` aloud as \"nums at index 2\". Indexes count **from 0**, which feels odd at first: the index is really a **distance from the start**, and the first element is zero steps away. The last element of an array of length `n` is therefore at index `n - 1`."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Creating an array"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "There are two common ways: list the values directly, or ask for a number of slots filled with a default value."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "const primes = [2, 3, 5, 7, 11];          // list the values\nconst scores = new Array(5).fill(0);      // 5 slots, all 0\nconst empty = [];                         // grows as you add\nconsole.log(primes.length);               // 5"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "primes = [2, 3, 5, 7, 11]                 # list the values\nscores = [0] * 5                          # 5 slots, all 0\nempty = []                                # grows as you add\nprint(len(primes))                        # 5"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int[] primes = {2, 3, 5, 7, 11};          // list the values\nint[] scores = new int[5];                // 5 slots, all 0 by default\nList<Integer> growable = new ArrayList<>();  // grows as you add\nSystem.out.println(primes.length);        // 5"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int primes[] = {2, 3, 5, 7, 11};          // fixed-size C-style array\nvector<int> scores(5, 0);                 // 5 slots, all 0\nvector<int> growable;                     // grows as you add\ncout << scores.size() << endl;            // 5"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In **Java** and **C++**, a plain array has a fixed size chosen when it's created. When you need an array that grows, use `ArrayList` (Java) or `vector` (C++). JavaScript arrays and Python lists grow on their own."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Reading and updating"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "const nums = [4, 1, 7, 3];\nconsole.log(nums[0]);                     // 4  (first)\nconsole.log(nums[nums.length - 1]);       // 3  (last)\nnums[2] = 9;                              // update in place\nconsole.log(nums);                        // [4, 1, 9, 3]"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "nums = [4, 1, 7, 3]\nprint(nums[0])                            # 4  (first)\nprint(nums[-1])                           # 3  (last: negative indexes count from the end)\nnums[2] = 9                               # update in place\nprint(nums)                               # [4, 1, 9, 3]"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int[] nums = {4, 1, 7, 3};\nSystem.out.println(nums[0]);                    // 4  (first)\nSystem.out.println(nums[nums.length - 1]);      // 3  (last)\nnums[2] = 9;                                    // update in place\nSystem.out.println(Arrays.toString(nums));      // [4, 1, 9, 3]"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "vector<int> nums = {4, 1, 7, 3};\ncout << nums[0] << endl;                        // 4  (first)\ncout << nums[nums.size() - 1] << endl;          // 3  (last)\nnums[2] = 9;                                    // update in place\nfor (int x : nums) cout << x << \" \";            // 4 1 9 3"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Reading or writing any index costs the same: **O(1)**. The computer never walks to index 2 — it calculates where index 2 lives, as you saw in the memory model lesson."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Adding and removing at the end"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Growable arrays can add and remove at the **end** cheaply, because nothing else has to move:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "const stack = [1, 2];\nstack.push(3);            // [1, 2, 3]\nconst last = stack.pop(); // last = 3, stack = [1, 2]"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "stack = [1, 2]\nstack.append(3)           # [1, 2, 3]\nlast = stack.pop()        # last = 3, stack = [1, 2]"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "List<Integer> stack = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 2));\nstack.add(3);                               // [1, 2, 3]\nint last = stack.remove(stack.size() - 1);  // last = 3, stack = [1, 2]"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "vector<int> stack = {1, 2};\nstack.push_back(3);        // {1, 2, 3}\nint last = stack.back();   // last = 3\nstack.pop_back();          // stack = {1, 2}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Adding or removing at the **front** or in the **middle** is a different story: every later element has to shift one place, so it costs **O(n)**. JavaScript's `unshift`/`shift`, Python's `insert(0, x)`/`pop(0)`, and `add(0, x)` on an `ArrayList` all look innocent but hide that loop."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The cost of each operation"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Read or write by index** — `nums[i]`: **O(1)**.",
        "**Add or remove at the end** — push/pop, append, add, push_back: **O(1)** on average.",
        "**Add or remove at the front or middle** — everything after it shifts: **O(n)**.",
        "**Search for a value** in an unsorted array — check each slot: **O(n)**.",
        "**Get the length** — stored alongside the array: **O(1)**."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Keep this list in mind. Nearly every optimisation in this course is about replacing an O(n) operation, repeated inside a loop, with an O(1) one."
    }
  ],
